Here is my whole face with this look. In the vein of previous posts it’s not great because my remote is missing and I had to take this in the bathroom mirror. I might as well rename the blog Mirror Tour 2k14. </s> At a time when BitTorrent sites are increasingly at odds with the music industry and file-sharing continues to flourish despite legal action, new thinking is called for. Instead of joining the conflict with BitTorrent sites, Honor Roll Music has decided to sponsor isoHunt instead, one of the leading torrent search engines.

Awesome New Republic is a two member indie band based in Miami, Florida. Both band members graduated at Frost School of Music and their first album, released in 2005, was well received by the critics. Together with their label Honor Roll Music they decided to try out quite an innovative marketing strategy to promote their latest album.

In order to get the word out, the label booked a monthly banner advertisement campaign on isoHunt, and linked that to a torrent file of the album that everyone can download for free. By doing so they hope to get people excited about their music and eventually build a strong fanbase.

“As far as we know, we are the first record label/band to advertise directly on a torrent search engine,” Colin Foord, the creative brain behind the campaign told TorrentFreak. “We are sponsoring isoHunt with a banner that links directly to our homepage where the album can be downloaded for free.”

Awesome New Republic’s banner on isoHunt

In a music industry that is changing quickly, Honor Roll Music recognizes that torrent sites such as isoHunt should not be vilified, but turned into partners instead in order to reach millions of music fans eager to discover and download new music. BitTorrent sites have proven to be a stepping stone for developing artists, rather than a threat.

With a limited marketing budget the label can’t afford to buy ad space on all major torrent sites, but the isoHunt sponsorship is a sign of good faith and shows that they do recognize the value of reaching out to the millions of active BitTorrent users.

The latest Awesome New Republic album titled “Rational Geographic Vol 1” is available on several torrent sites, and everyone is free to download a copy and share it with friends. Volume II will be released in July and Volume III, with a physical 3-volume bundle, in October. </s> Electric vehicle racing series Formula E has announced a new sponsorship agreement with the EMEA division of Disney.

Under the terms of the deal, the electric street racing series will help to promote Disney subsidiary Pixar’s new movie, Cars 3. The collaboration began at the Qatar Airways Paris ePrix on 20th May, and saw the media and entertainment giant benefit from extensive branding around the race circuit.

Additionally, the agreement will involve various trackside activations with the aim of engaging the next generation of sports fans, while both parties will be producing a series of content involving Formula E drivers which will feature across both organisations’ digital platforms.

Ben Padley, global marketing and brand director at Formula E, said: “Teaming up with Disney epitomises the uniqueness of the FIA Formula E Championship and our ability to engage the next generation of sports fans.

“Formula E races in the centre of the most iconic cities in the world and our collaboration with Disney is a truly authentic way to showcase our electric street racing series and the raw driving skills of our drivers.”

Tricia Wilber, chief marketing officer at The Walt Disney Company EMEA, said: “The Cars franchise and Formula E complement one another perfectly in creating an authentic and meaningful partnership, and we look forward to a great long-term collaboration between us.

“The drivers in the series embody the dedication and determination also seen with our characters and enable us to highlight the key qualities which anyone can use to be a champion.”

The 2016/17 FIA Formula E Championship continues with the Berlin ePrix in Germany this Saturday. </s> Yesterday, the Nuclear Regulatory Commission issued a final rule creating a variable fee structure for small, light water reactors. It creates a new term called a "bundled unit" and then bases the annual fee on the thermal power output of the bundled unit. It is a three part structure with a minimum fee, a variable fee computed as a function of thermal power, and a maximum fee for a range of output power.

Background On Annual Fees

Nearly eight years ago the Nuclear Regulatory formally recognized that its existing annual license fee structure might be unfair and inequitable for licensees that operate reactors that produce far less power than the existing fleet of behemoths, the least powerful of which is large enough to produce sufficient electricity for the needs and wants of moderate sized city of 400,000 people.

During the Reagan Administration, as part of David Stockman's idea to reduce taxes and replace the income with user fees, the NRC was converted from a public service regulatory agency supported by general revenues to a regulator supported by fees on the industry it regulated. The specific legislation that imposed this change was the Omnibus Budget Reconciliation Act of 1990 (OBRA-90), which has been slightly amended several times during the past 26 years.

Under the rule currently in effect, every reactor pays the identical annual fee. The charge for FY 2015 is $5,030,000.

When all reactors were large, extra large, and double extra large the fee was already moderately unfair and inequitable to the reactors that were only L and not XL or XXL. The amount of the flat fee has been one of the many factors that have encouraged early shutdowns of less big reactors like Kewaunee and Vermont Yankee, the announced closure of Pilgrim, and the almost final decision to close Ft. Calhoun.

Once a reactor operator informs the NRC that it has ceased operations permanently and has removed all of the fuel from a reactor, the $5 million/yr operating license is converted into a possession only license that has an annual NRC fee of just $223,000.

A fee difference of >$4.7 million/year provides a substantial incentive for operators of extra large plants that are shut down due to economic challenges to quickly move to put reactors into a state where it is virtually impossible to refurbish and restore them when market prices change.

Along with payroll savings from sloughing off a large portion of a well-paid work force and the reduction in "excess" power generation that had been helping to reduce coal and natural gas demand while also keeping electricity prices low, several large power producers have decided that closing existing nukes is a no brainer.

There is nothing really unusual about commodity producers adjusting production capacity to better match supply to demand; one illogical aspect of this action under our current market conditions is that some of the electricity factories that supply the highest quality product at a cost that is predictable and affordable over the long term are the plants that are being closed.

The other illogical aspect is that those closed nuclear plants cannot be mothballed; they get destroyed instead. Under current conditions, replacing them with another plant that can produce power at the same level of quality will cost several billion dollars and take at least ten years for planning, licensing and construction.

Annual Fees For Advanced Reactors

One of the innovative changes in thinking that occurred as a result of careful analysis of the lessons available from the demise of the first Atomic Age was a recognition that "economy of scale" did not mean that only the largest machines could survive.

Starting as early as 1990, there has been a growing interest in building right-sized reactors that could serve a wider variety of customers and even begin to find customers interested in using fission heat for something besides generating electricity.

The flat fee structure described above was one of many artificial obstacles to building and selling smaller reactors. For example, a 10 MWe power plant aiming to serve all of the power needs for a compact Alaskan village like Galena might operate at a 50% capacity factor, not because it is unreliable, but because the demand varies. It would therefore produce ~50,000 MW-hrs of electricity. By itself, the NRC fee would represent an ongoing operating cost of $100/MW-hr - 10 cents per kilowatt-hour.

In 2008, with credible potential vendors telling the NRC that they were seriously working on designs for machines as small as a few megawatts of thermal power, the staff decided it was time to consider a rulemaking. The document docket number assigned for the project was NRC–2008–0664.

The NRC issued an Advanced Notice of Proposed Rulemaking (ANPR) in May 2009 that requested answers to six questions. The questions covered the need for a variable fee structure, possible basis for a new fee structure, the treatment of multiple reactors on a single site and fees for reactors that generate heat for purposes other than generating electricity. The last question was an open ended one asking if there were any other factors to consider. Sixteen entities provided comments.

As one of the entities that provided comments on that ANPR, via a letter from Adams Atomic Engines, Inc. dated May 31, 2009, I can testify that there was no indication in the ANPR that the agency was considering limiting the rule revisions to light water reactors. AAE, Inc. was not the only responder whose technology was a non-light water reactor.

After full staff analysis of the public comments, consideration of point papers from the Nuclear Energy Institute and American Nuclear Society and internal analysis by a working group, the NRC's Chief Financial Officer submitted a memo to the Commission titled "Resolution of Issue Regarding Variable Annual Fee Structure for Small and Medium-Sized Nuclear Power Reactors" dated Feb 7, 2011.

After March 11, 2011, the Commission dedicated all available resources to its [over]reaction to the Fukushima Frenzy. No action was taken on the CFO's memo until FY 2014.

The NRC issued a draft rule in November 2015. That draft rule included a new definition of small modular reactor.

Small modular reactor (SMR) for the purposes of calculating fees, means the class of light-water power reactors having a licensed thermal power rating less than or equal to 1,000 MWt per module. This rating is based on the thermal power equivalent of a light-water SMR with an electrical power generating capacity of 300 MWe or less per module.

The NRC received nine comments on that draft rule, made some administrative changes and rejected important comments from both Per Peterson of the University of California, Berkeley (UCB) and Tyler Ellis of MIT. Both of those innovators suggested that the rule should not be artificially constrained by limiting it to light water reactors.

Evidence That NRC Is Not Ready To Review Non-LWR Design

With the issuance of the final rule published in the Federal Register on May 24, 2016 and going into effect on June 23, 2016, the NRC has declared that it will need another rulemaking before it can apply the new fee schedule to non-light water reactors. Here is the explanation provided for rejecting the suggestions from Dr. Peterson and Dr. Ellis.

The NRC disagrees with the comment. The NRC has chosen to limit the scope of this proposed rule to lightwater SMRs. This is because the lightwater SMR designs that have been discussed with the NRC in preapplication discussions to date are similar to the current U.S. operating fleet of reactors in terms of physical configuration, operational characteristics, and applicability to the NRC’s existing regulatory framework. The NRC may consider the inclusion of non-light water SMRs in a future rulemaking once the agency has increased understanding of these factors with respect to non-light water designs. No change was made to the final rule in response to this comment.

Dr. Peterson was disappointed with the decision.

This provides an example of how the NRC can create regulatory uncertainty for advanced reactor applicants, that will take a long time for the first advanced reactor applicant to resolve (since it requires rulemaking), where competitors who apply for licenses afterward can free ride on the cost and risk that the first mover must bear.

So was Dr. Ellis.

“All this serves to do is leave in place additional regulatory barriers for promising new nuclear energy technologies from entering the US market…I don't follow the NRCs logic of the need to "better understand non-light water designs" in order to make a correction to the NRC fee structure.”

Caroline Cochran of Oklo, a start-up company that expects to be ready to submit a design certification application to the NRC well before 2020, was hopeful and optimistic. She and her co-founder like the fact that the minimum fee in the new rule is anchored to the fees currently paid by research reactors. She thinks that the rule sets a precedent that will make it possible to apply similar logic to non-LWR reactors.

Unfortunately, there is a contentious and lengthy process associated with an NRC rulemaking. The one that is the subject of this post took eight years. Three of the companies that submitted comments on the first ANPR have either gone out of business or significantly repositioned themselves to produce different products. The inability of the NRC to make simple changes to remove artificial barriers was largely responsible for the failure of at least one of those companies.

NRC Commissioners have testified in front of both House and Senate committees that they are ready to engage with reactor vendors proposing advanced reactors that do not use conventional light water technology. Though the designs being proposed today are thoroughly modern, incorporate lessons that have been learned both inside and outside of nuclear technology and may be proposed by companies with innovative business, manufacturing and construction paradigms, they are all using basic concepts that have been around as long as fission has been around.

There is nothing new or unusual about reactors that use molten metal coolants, high temperature gases, heavy water, or molten salt even for the NRC.

Ft. St. Vrain and Peach Bottom 1 were both licensed and operated for a decade as high temperature gas cooled reactors. Fermi 1 was a licensed and operated liquid metal cooled reactor.

More recently the NRC has performed detailed work in the past on liquid metal cooled reactors for GE's PRISM and has been funded to work on regulatory frameworks for high temperature gas reactors under the NGNP program.

It would have been simple and efficient to simply maintain the technology agnostic definition of small modular reactor. As one of the long time proponents of SMRs, I'd never given any thought to the possibility that the regulators would decide to limit the purposely broad term to a single technology.

During a speech given to the U.S. Energy Association on May 5, 2016, NRC Chairman Stephen Burns made the following statement.

While being prepared to evaluate potential applications for light water-based small modular reactors and non-light water reactor technologies presents some challenges for the NRC, the agency is ready to receive and review any such applications under our existing framework. To be clear, the NRC has the necessary licensing and oversight authority over commercial advanced reactors, and is ready to work with potential applicants to prepare for and review applications for these reactors. No new legislation is required.

While I agree that the NRC has the necessary authority, I cannot agree that the agency is ready to work with applicants and review applications for these reactors. There is too much financial risk associated with entering into the formal review process without some predictability about the regulatory cost burden imposed.

The annual license fee is only one of several issues where the NRC must use its authority to make rules clear, fair and equitable. It should be able to determine the magnitude of the regulatory effort associated with technologies that have already been proven through physical testing to have passive safety features that are equal to or superior to advanced light water reactors.

The crying need for additional legislation is to give the NRC the responsibility of enabling nuclear technologies to safely flourish to match its legislated monopoly authority that has helped to prevent them from flourishing through inaction or painfully expensive processes. </s> Interested in purchasing Artkal Beads, but not sure which colors to get? Let us help!

